{"kind":"assert","text":"Stage 5 acceptance clarification: historical declarations and generic check transports.\n\nHistorical readers. UI, client and MCP render each record with the label, targets and fields of the retained D(s) at that record's sequence, as R-DECL-23 requires. Current declarations are for preparing new intent, not for interpreting old records. Show the sequence at which an old kind was retired; later reuse of the same name does not erase that history or relabel/reinterpret the old record. Retained policy identity and binding disambiguate reused names. This includes label/help-only edits with unchanged binding. Stage 5 owns any authenticated historical-declaration read it needs and the UI/client/MCP consumption; coordinate the Room route seam explicitly with reviewed stage 2. Cover activation, retirement, later name reuse with changed shape, label-only change, legacy records and exact old retries. Preserve all existing stage-5 scope and tests.\n\nTransport decision. Keep the legacy v1 rule: a bearer check uses bearerAct over RPC; no named MCP check tool is added; the legacy HTTPS bearer client's fixed check method remains forbidden, and bearer roster is always forbidden. For generic v2, permit the explicit bound generic act to run a declared check step over the MCP endpoint, including through a bearer HTTPS client whose generic act routes to that endpoint. The caller supplies kind, target, body, binding and idempotency key; the room uses only that session's key/delegation and never silently replaces the binding. The delegation's signed grant map must cover that exact kind and binding, the current role/declaration must permit it, and every ordinary admission/step-check guard and R-OBL-3 qualification applies. This is an intentional amendment of R-CRED-10's transport wording, whose former HTTPS exclusion arose from the absence of a check tool; it grants no new authority merely because a name changes. Judge the check primitive, not only the literal string check. Do not accept v1 checks through the generic v2 path or bearer acts on the ordinary signed-envelope POST /acts route. Key-signed HTTPS/client checks remain available under their existing authority and exact v2 binding rules.\n\nStage 5 owns this generic transport seam, including the affected protocol text (R-CRED-10, R-API-9 and any corresponding type/API wording), all changed artifacts, and allowed/refused path tests at the same implementation head. Tests cover a differently named declared check act, granted and ungranted/stale bindings, insufficient role, v1 and roster exclusion, fixed legacy client behavior, explicit generic client routing, exact retry, and the holder/proposer who cannot meet their own check obligation. Preserve exact generation/job/integration/configuration/input binding; a generic submission or a claimant's own check is not independent checker evidence. Stage 4 still owns production CheckJob v2 mapping, checker-service signing/routing and prepared facts; allowing a transport does not implement that service path.\n\nThe first jam development task may use independent reviews through generic acts, as builder's judgment states. It need not depend on this generic machine-check path or automated checker-service checks. Builder retains readiness ownership; every implementation retains its full acceptance and exact-head review. This metadata clarification approves no source.","body":{"implementation":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a5d64b35e87c97560ff354578d9560bd1261aacb","no_git_artifact":"true"}}